/-!
# Transcendental j forces every isogeny endomorphism to be an integer

Theorem statement:
`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.IsogenyEndDatum.exists_forall_pointEnd_eq_zsmul_of_transcendental_j`
(`6dd2c674-31b5-5cc3-a8b5-7b0ca8e877c2`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `6070a9c7-1ec2-518c-ace4-b2ff7e195a0c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and $W$ an elliptic Weierstrass
curve over $F$, equipped with the three structural hypotheses of the project's divisor-theoretic
framework for $W$ in affine form: a `GenusOnePlaceGate`, i.e. a bijection between the points
$W.\mathrm{Point}$ and the places of the function field $F(W)$ over $F$, all of which have degree
$1$; the centring condition `GenusOnePlaceGate.IsCentred`, asserting that for every nonsingular pair
$(x,y)$ the images in $F(W)$ of the coordinate-ring classes of $X - x$ and of $Y - y$ lie in the
nonunits of the valuation subring of the place attached to the point $(x,y)$; and `AbelTheorem`,
asserting that a divisor of degree $0$ on $F(W)$ is principal exactly when its point-sum vanishes.
Assume further that every isogeny endomorphism datum $D$ of $W$ — a pair consisting of an integral
$F$-algebra endomorphism $\iota$ of $F(W)$ making $F(W)$ a finite module over itself along $\iota$ —
satisfies the pushforward norm formula along $\iota$, i.e. the divisor pushforward of the divisor of
any nonzero $f \in F(W)$ is the divisor of $\mathrm{N}(f)$. Finally assume $W.j$ is transcendental
over $\mathbb{Q}$. Then for every isogeny endomorphism datum $D_0$ there is an integer $m$ with
$D_0.\mathrm{pointEnd}(P) = m \cdot P$ for all $P \in W.\mathrm{Point}$, where $\mathrm{pointEnd}$
is the endomorphism of the group of points induced, via the isomorphism with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, by
pushforward along $\iota$.

This is the statement that an elliptic curve with transcendental $j$-invariant has no complex
multiplication: its ring of isogeny endomorphisms, as realised here by pushforward along finite
self-embeddings of the function field, reduces to $\mathbb{Z}$. It is used in the treatment of
modular polynomials and of Tate points, in particular for identifying the subgroups generated by a
point and for injectivity statements on kernels.

